sub:assertion {
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/approach/bioelectric-prepattern-predicts-anatomy--grok-4-7--3> a prov:Plan, delphi:TestableHypothesis ;
    rdfs:label "Prove which Vmem summaries cannot identify anatomy" ;
    dct:subject <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/problem/bioelectric-prepattern-predicts-anatomy> ;
    dct:description "A decoder that sees only the anterior-posterior dipole and the number of supra-threshold domains assigns the same phenotype to any two fields that agree on those summaries. Prove that statement. Then apply it to the admissible figures: search for two experiments with different caption phenotypes whose dipole and domain count agree within a tolerance frozen first (relative dipole difference below 0.1, same domain count). A hit refutes every decoder that uses only those features. Absence of a hit does not confirm the bioelectric code; it only says these features are not yet contradicted by the figures. This approach can refute a feature set that the classifier approach would merely score as inaccurate." ;

    delphi:proposedTest "Either a pair of published experiments with different phenotypes and summaries inside the tolerance, which refutes dipole-and-domain decoders, or an exhaustive statement that no such pair exists in the admissible figures." ;
    delphi:context "Fails if: The lemma is false for some pair of fields, which would be a mathematics error, or the figure measurements are too coarse to decide equality within 0.1, in which case the empirical half is inconclusive." ;
    delphi:hypothesisType "approach/formalization" ;
    skos:note "Steps: 1. Prove the identifiability lemma for dipole and domain count. 2. Freeze the 0.1 relative tolerance. 3. Measure the summaries on the admissible figures. 4. Search for a cross-phenotype collision under that tolerance. 5. Report a refuting pair or the absence of one." ;
    skos:note "Difficulty (proposer's estimate of completion within about a month of agent time): tractable" ;
    skos:note "Missing: Raw pixel values. Figure measurements may be too coarse for the 0.1 tolerance, and the approach then stops as inconclusive on the empirical half." ;
    skos:note "Tool: lean4" ;
    skos:note "Tool: image-analysis" ;
    skos:related <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/levin-2015-girardia-11> ;
    skos:related <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/levin-2018-hcn2-06> ;
    skos:related <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/levin-2018-hcn2-16> ;
    skos:related <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/levin-2019-boundary-14> ;

    rdfs:seeAlso <https://doi.org/10.1016/j.biosystems.2017.08.009> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://doi.org/10.1016/j.bpj.2017.04.011> ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://github.com/fractastical/delphi/blob/ee98025a1960b300fcc06aa2106f51118b60f565/problems/approaches/grok-4-7.json> .
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/problem/bioelectric-prepattern-predicts-anatomy> a delphi:Concept, schema:Question ;
    rdfs:label "Does the bioelectric prepattern predict anatomical outcome?" .


  <https://doi.org/10.1016/j.biosystems.2017.08.009> dct:title "The bioelectric code: An ancient computational medium for dynamic control of growth and form" ;
    dct:bibliographicCitation "Levin and Martyniuk 2018, the mapping this lemma constrains" .
  <https://doi.org/10.1016/j.bpj.2017.04.011> dct:title "Long-Term, Stochastic Editing of Regenerative Anatomy via Targeting Endogenous Bioelectric Gradients" ;
    dct:bibliographicCitation "Durant et al. 2017, planarian figure set searched for a colliding pair" .
}

